#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084277} :activity/id #uuid "38aff8ec-fcf4-52b8-8557-cb2bcff71738" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048719 :topic/id #uuid "b8656b86-9f88-52e9-adb0-d3cc15fbb7cc" :topic/math-academy-id 755} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084484 :tutorial/content "The **lowest common multiple** (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of two integers is the smallest multiple that's common to both numbers.\n\nTo find the lowest common multiple of two small numbers, we can list all multiples of the two numbers and identify the smallest multiple that they have in common.\n\nFor example, let's find the lowest common multiple of $4$ and $6.$ We start by listing all multiples of $4$ and $6\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & \\,\\to \\,4,\\,8,\\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]12[/math]}},\\,16,\\,20,\\,24,\\,… \\\\ 6 & \\,\\to \\,6,\\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]12[/math]}},\\,18,\\,24,\\,30,\\,36,\\,…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe smallest multiple in both lists is ![](images/92/9285d37c7b455395acb42a54f78348bedfd8c9a72ad63d6e6ab7c2426148ea0d.svg) So $12$ is our answer, and we can write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(4,6)= 12.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The lowest common multiple is also sometimes called the *least* common multiple." :tutorial/id #uuid "b16f2464-2c19-5166-bd61-97043695afcb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5301 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "853eea8e-7e8c-5972-b2df-6a996d469c6a" :step/math-academy-id 33113 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084281}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083330 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083132 :question/id #uuid "f524667d-8cd2-5b91-a9e0-538fc64399e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8869"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "384f8f04-972d-50cb-a023-dba29924242e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050212}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493920}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lowest Common Multiple of Two Integers"} :step/id #uuid "0c8932d3-46c6-5cd6-941d-98887b4bfb0e" :step/math-academy-id 33116 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084280}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084279 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083331 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083133 :question/id #uuid "7c928766-9971-565a-b38b-5c97b2c3ee1d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8870"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c28dd57d-e6e5-5704-94a4-5f9bfb37f383" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493965}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lowest Common Denominator of Two Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "764cac2a-73a8-581d-8ccd-4d665b951e2b" :step/math-academy-id 33118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084280 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083332 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083134 :question/id #uuid "1373d2e3-fc83-56f2-b779-8f1e2d10efea" :question/math-academy-id "e-8874"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "746e99f3-561d-5212-ba2a-3d34b8bcdbee" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493959}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lowest Common Multiple of Three Integers"} :step/id #uuid "7e705e91-32b9-5486-b6d0-c6ebfbc45c9b" :step/math-academy-id 33136 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084279}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084281 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084485 :tutorial/content "Now suppose that we want to find the lowest common multiple of two larger numbers. It would take a while to make long lists of multiples. Luckily, there is a faster method. \n\nLet's demonstrate by finding $\\textrm{LCM}(10,12).$ We start by writing the prime factorization of $10$ and $12$, writing any repeating factors as powers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =2\\cdot 5={2}^{1}\\cdot {5}^{1} \\\\ 12 & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3={2}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write down each unique prime factor that appears in either of our decompositions. In our case, the unique prime factors are \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2}, \\qquad {\\color{red}3}, \\qquad {\\color{red}5}.\n$$\n\nNow, for each number in our list, we look at *both* prime factorizations and we take the *largest* power shown for each prime factor:\n\n- The factors corresponding to ${\\color{red}2}$ are ${\\color{red}2}^{\\color{blue}1}$ and ${\\color{red}2}^{\\color{blue}2}.$ Of these, the larger power is ${\\color{red}2}^{\\color{blue}2}.$\n\n- There is only one factor corresponding to ${\\color{red}3}.$ It is ${\\color{red}3}^{\\color{blue}1}.$\n\n- There is only one factor corresponding to ${\\color{red}5}.$ It is ${\\color{red}5}^{\\color{blue}1}.$\n\nFinally, we multiply the largest powers of each factor together, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(10,12) & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{1}\\cdot {5}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "de44c642-c9d2-5c1d-8b59-8781a99559ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5489 :tutorial/title "Finding the Lowest Common Multiple of Two Larger Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "44919f35-fd65-50af-b639-7d5eb83ab3b0" :step/math-academy-id 34200 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084278}}] :activity/title "Finding Lowest Common Multiples Using Prime Factorization" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083445}]}
